<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Bulala Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1350 CE – 1900 CE (approx.) · capital Yao</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Muslim dynasty of the Lake Fitri region that drove the Sayfawa kings out of Kanem in the 14th century and survived as the Sultanate of Fitri.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulala">Bulala</a> were a people around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-fitri">Lake Fitri</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a> whose ruling house was related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayfawa-dynasty">Sayfawa dynasty</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem-Bornu Empire</a>. In the second half of the 14th century they waged repeated wars on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kanem">Kanem</a>, killing several Sayfawa kings and forcing the dynasty to abandon its capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/njimi">Njimi</a> and move west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>For over a century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulala">Bulala</a> ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kanem">Kanem</a> from bases near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-fitri">Lake Fitri</a>, controlling trade routes between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a> basin. Their state was a Muslim sultanate supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>-keeping and agricultural subjects. Few internal details survive; most information comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a> chronicles hostile to them.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idris-katakarmabi">Idris Katakarmabi</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulala">Bulala</a> in the early 16th century and reoccupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/njimi">Njimi</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idris-alooma">Idris Alooma</a> later pushed them back further. The Bulala retreated to Fitri, later paying tribute to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/wadai-empire">Wadai Empire</a>. The Sultanate of Fitri at Yao came under French rule around 1900 and survives as a traditional authority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="sultanate, 1350 CE – 1900 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">sultanate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:86.57%;width:10.95%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1350 CE – 1900 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yao</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bilala, Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Chad</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bulala, Sultanate of Fitri, Fitri</dd></dl></aside></div>

